Failure evidence links history, mechanism and alternatives (@mat-material-failure: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #materials-engineering "../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/service/Failure-investigation-preserves-chronology-and-alternatives.lingua#r_2YM12BJW2NZH0A7FAC0K9DZC68") { r_6JQNH0AAKYQB85CXPHJJBXK1Q6
**Read:** The shared mechanical failure-investigation Record and its complete references; revisit fracture, environmental damage and manufacturing defects.

An observed fracture surface is part of a chronology. Separate initiation, growth, final overload and changes introduced during recovery or examination. A feature compatible with a mechanism may not uniquely establish it. Loading, microstructure, residual stress, environment and geometry can cooperate.

Preserve competing explanations long enough to test them. Compare failed and unfailed populations without assuming their histories match. A cause at material scale may reveal a process, design, inspection or operating condition elsewhere in the system. Replacing the broken part alone need not remove that condition.

**Work:** Reconstruct a published failure case from service history through specimen observations and model predictions. State which evidence discriminates between two plausible mechanisms. Propose a material/process change, then explain what new evidence would show whether it addresses the cause. Distinguish an inference from an observation and a demonstrated prevention from a proposal.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
